00:22:57.460 --> 00:22:59.578
getting back to the level of immunity

00:22:59.578 --> 00:23:00.411
and how long it lasts,

00:23:00.411 --> 00:23:03.460
it's a possibility that
vaccination will be effective

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:04.883
for a period of time,

00:23:04.883 --> 00:23:06.864
but that will need to re-vaccinate

00:23:06.864 --> 00:23:08.638
in a year or two or three.

00:23:08.638 --> 00:23:09.638
We just don't know yet

00:23:09.638 --> 00:23:11.258
because we haven't had any time.

00:23:11.258 --> 00:23:14.338
So there are different
possibilities in our future,

00:23:14.338 --> 00:23:16.978
but so far, the good news is
it looks like the vaccines

00:23:16.978 --> 00:23:19.508
that are being created
will work for all lineages,

00:23:19.508 --> 00:23:21.264
if they they work.

00:23:21.264 --> 00:23:24.478
- So it sounds like an optimistic scenario

00:23:24.478 --> 00:23:27.748
would be things start
to somewhat normalize

00:23:27.748 --> 00:23:30.208
in the spring, summer of next year.

00:23:30.208 --> 00:23:31.818
Although that's kind
of still of optimistic.

00:23:31.818 --> 00:23:34.778
That would be if we are
able to get 40 million folks

00:23:34.778 --> 00:23:36.808
in the US alone with the vaccines,

00:23:36.808 --> 00:23:39.308
you know, the February, March timeframe.

00:23:39.308 --> 00:23:40.908
And then obviously you
need to get many more

00:23:40.908 --> 00:23:42.882
to get true herd immunity.

00:23:42.882 --> 00:23:44.157
And I wanna go...

00:23:44.157 --> 00:23:47.198
These conversations always go
a lot faster than I expected.

00:23:47.198 --> 00:23:48.428
I definitely want to get to the

00:23:48.428 --> 00:23:50.208
idea of school closures and universities,

00:23:50.208 --> 00:23:51.988
but the one last question about,

00:23:51.988 --> 00:23:55.470
you know, things that make
the virus itself less scary.

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:58.428
You know, the other track
is on the therapeutic side,

00:23:58.428 --> 00:24:00.138
you know, how do we make
the virus less deadly,

00:24:00.138 --> 00:24:02.312
less likely to do permanent harm to folks?

00:24:02.312 --> 00:24:04.368
Where do you see our journey there?

00:24:04.368 --> 00:24:05.548
I've heard things like, you know,

00:24:05.548 --> 00:24:07.648
some of these anti-inflammatory drugs,

00:24:07.648 --> 00:24:11.198
you know, the steroids, you
know, preventing the cytokine.

00:24:11.198 --> 00:24:14.198
My wife's a rheumatologist,
so I hear a lot about this.

00:24:15.244 --> 00:24:20.244
Where do you see that line of reasoning

00:24:21.408 --> 00:24:24.352
going over the next eight, nine months?

00:24:24.352 --> 00:24:28.440
So, so far the therapeutic
that's had the greatest benefit

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:30.228
has been oral steroids,

00:24:30.228 --> 00:24:32.458
something glucocorticoids or steroids

00:24:32.458 --> 00:24:33.698
that the medicines we use

00:24:33.698 --> 00:24:36.468
for all sorts of common
inflammatory diseases.

00:24:36.468 --> 00:24:39.735
And that's had a pretty
substantial benefit

00:24:39.735 --> 00:24:43.338
in people who are intubated,
the sickest people.

00:24:43.338 --> 00:24:47.508
It showed as much as a third
reduction in mortality,

00:24:47.508 --> 00:24:51.256
which is pretty dramatic for
one medical intervention.

00:24:51.256 --> 00:24:54.838
We have an antiviral,
which is from Remdesivir,

00:24:54.838 --> 00:24:57.418
which is much smaller benefit.

00:24:57.418 --> 00:25:00.460
It's kind of, it's believed at a benefit,

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:02.451
but not as substantial one.

00:25:04.018 --> 00:25:07.414
And so far, you know, everything
else is still in trials.

00:25:07.414 --> 00:25:11.668
There are antibodies that
are engineered antibodies

00:25:11.668 --> 00:25:12.858
that are being studied.

00:25:12.858 --> 00:25:14.938
And hopefully we'll know
by the end of the year,

00:25:14.938 --> 00:25:18.648
there's a lot of hope that they
might also reduce mortality

00:25:18.648 --> 00:25:20.828
perhaps used in combination
with other medicines,

00:25:20.828 --> 00:25:23.390
like Remdesivir or Dexamethasone.

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:26.481
So hopeful that those
things will also contribute

00:25:26.481 --> 00:25:29.697
to overall lessening mortality.

00:25:29.697 --> 00:25:33.238
We're learning things
about the care of patients

00:25:33.238 --> 00:25:34.968
in the intensive care unit

00:25:34.968 --> 00:25:38.838
about trying to, you know,
either turn them over in a way

00:25:38.838 --> 00:25:40.966
that improves their oxygenation

00:25:40.966 --> 00:25:45.966
or trying to avoid ventilators
for longer if possible,

00:25:47.998 --> 00:25:50.168
because that makes it a
little bit more difficult

00:25:50.168 --> 00:25:52.668
to care for people and
to have good outcomes.

00:25:52.668 --> 00:25:54.153
So I think if you look at the overall way

00:25:54.153 --> 00:25:57.293
that people are cared for,
with this disease in the US

00:25:57.293 --> 00:26:00.174
there has been a clear
improvement over time.

00:26:00.174 --> 00:26:02.278
And hopefully that will
continue to get better

00:26:02.278 --> 00:26:04.661
as we have more things
come through the system.

00:26:05.738 --> 00:26:07.718
- But, you know, if I
read between the lines,

00:26:07.718 --> 00:26:08.598
it sounds like that's good.

00:26:08.598 --> 00:26:10.938
And we have to be doing that
as much of that as possible,

00:26:10.938 --> 00:26:12.868
but it's really going to be the vaccine

00:26:12.868 --> 00:26:13.868
that will normalize things

00:26:13.868 --> 00:26:15.918
unless we have some kind
of unexpected breakthrough

00:26:15.918 --> 00:26:17.680
on the therapeutic side.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:19.548
- Yeah, even if we have a breakthrough

00:26:19.548 --> 00:26:22.118
in the therapeutic side that's dramatic,

00:26:22.118 --> 00:26:23.636
it's not really going to change

00:26:23.636 --> 00:26:25.898
unless it was available to everyone.

00:26:25.898 --> 00:26:28.448
And, you know, it's hard
to imagine conditions

00:26:28.448 --> 00:26:30.748
in which it would dramatically change

00:26:30.748 --> 00:26:32.468
the pattern of spread of the disease.

00:26:32.468 --> 00:26:35.258
It could change the outcome
perhaps dramatically,

00:26:35.258 --> 00:26:37.788
but not the overall
course of the pandemic.

00:26:37.788 --> 00:26:40.128
That's gonna require a vaccine.

00:26:40.128 --> 00:26:41.978
- Right, and there's a
world where the you know,

00:26:41.978 --> 00:26:44.743
if we had proper therapies,
the disease might not be,

00:26:44.743 --> 00:26:46.718
as you know, might be like the flu,

00:26:46.718 --> 00:26:48.028
but it sounds like we're far

00:26:48.028 --> 00:26:50.921
from that type of situation.
- At this point we are.

00:26:50.921 --> 00:26:52.738
- It wouldn't be everyone
wouldn't have access.

00:26:52.738 --> 00:26:55.048
So going to the school
opening and closures,

00:26:55.048 --> 00:26:56.688
and especially on the university level,

00:26:56.688 --> 00:26:58.158
you know, last week we had the chancellor

00:26:58.158 --> 00:27:01.028
from University of Illinois
Urbana-Champaign on,

00:27:01.028 --> 00:27:04.858
and they're doing a very
interesting operation experiment

00:27:04.858 --> 00:27:05.781
where they're doing,

00:27:05.781 --> 00:27:08.308
the whole campus is getting
tested every three days.

00:27:08.308 --> 00:27:09.378
They had developed their own test

00:27:09.378 --> 00:27:12.558
where they can get it within
hours, at 24 hours at most.

00:27:12.558 --> 00:27:14.788
They're doing really
strong contact tracing.

00:27:14.788 --> 00:27:17.168
What do you think about that?

00:27:17.168 --> 00:27:18.788
Do you think that is a solution

00:27:18.788 --> 00:27:20.628
for the next nine or 10 months?

00:27:20.628 --> 00:27:21.755
Or do you think that,

00:27:21.755 --> 00:27:23.565
or you will wait and see?

00:27:23.565 --> 00:27:26.648
- Well, I think we're going to find out,

00:27:26.648 --> 00:27:28.350
hopefully in the next couple of months,

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:30.313
because schools are taking a wide range

00:27:30.313 --> 00:27:32.178
of differing approaches

00:27:32.178 --> 00:27:35.564
and some schools are really
only testing sick kids

00:27:35.564 --> 00:27:37.178
and sick faculty members,

00:27:37.178 --> 00:27:39.058
people who clearly have symptoms

00:27:39.058 --> 00:27:40.518
and others, like you just said,

00:27:40.518 --> 00:27:42.687
are taking kind of the other,

00:27:42.687 --> 00:27:45.868
you know, kind of the opposite
pole, testing before arrival

00:27:45.868 --> 00:27:48.047
testing one, three, five days

00:27:48.047 --> 00:27:50.329
after arrival, a contact tracing.

00:27:50.329 --> 00:27:54.838
My instinct is that those
schools with much more testing

00:27:54.838 --> 00:27:56.808
and with really close contact tracing

00:27:56.808 --> 00:27:57.868
are gonna do much better,

00:27:57.868 --> 00:27:59.198
just like in the rest of society,

00:27:59.198 --> 00:28:02.207
that's kind of a closed
microcosm of society.

00:28:02.207 --> 00:28:03.708
We don't know for sure,

00:28:03.708 --> 00:28:04.868
coz this has never been tried,

00:28:04.868 --> 00:28:07.108
but I think we will learn hopefully,

00:28:07.108 --> 00:28:09.128
you know, in the next month or two.

00:28:09.128 --> 00:28:11.297
And so that universities that have taken

00:28:11.297 --> 00:28:13.907
a kind of a virtual approach
in the first semester,

00:28:13.907 --> 00:28:17.624
hopefully it become pretty
clear what the best practices

00:28:17.624 --> 00:28:19.327
are for going in January.

00:28:19.327 --> 00:28:22.048
Right now, I think there hasn't been data.

00:28:22.048 --> 00:28:25.717
I think the CDC has said they
didn't recommend approach.

00:28:25.717 --> 00:28:28.656
They didn't recommend testing
base symptomatics on arrival.

00:28:28.656 --> 00:28:32.608
I think many universities have
decided to do that anyway.

00:28:32.608 --> 00:28:34.508
And we'll see if it's effective.

00:28:34.508 --> 00:28:36.838
And if we have the bandwidth to do that,

00:28:36.838 --> 00:28:37.671
that's the other challenge,

00:28:37.671 --> 00:28:41.028
is that some universities
have incredible S and T,

00:28:41.028 --> 00:28:42.818
science and technology
within the university

00:28:42.818 --> 00:28:46.214
and can build those systems
while smaller schools,

00:28:46.214 --> 00:28:48.578
they may struggle to be able
to get that kind of testing

00:28:48.578 --> 00:28:50.928
that frequency and that,
you know, that extent.

00:28:52.299 --> 00:28:54.937
- If you know what I'm
sure you're advising

00:28:54.937 --> 00:28:57.023
the leadership at Johns Hopkins,

00:28:57.023 --> 00:28:59.408
but if you're a university
president right now,

00:28:59.408 --> 00:29:00.548
I guess you have three options,

00:29:00.548 --> 00:29:03.358
you can say essentially
pretty much distance learning

00:29:03.358 --> 00:29:04.388
or a little bit of hybrid,

00:29:04.388 --> 00:29:06.638
but, you know, that's
kind of the safest option.

00:29:06.638 --> 00:29:10.188
Option two seems like what
university at Illinois is doing,

00:29:10.188 --> 00:29:12.298
which is still, you know, very aggressive

00:29:12.298 --> 00:29:14.528
and obviously, you know,
places like Johns Hopkins

00:29:14.528 --> 00:29:16.008
obviously have the capacity to do,

00:29:16.008 --> 00:29:18.438
I would imagine similar types of things

00:29:18.438 --> 00:29:21.148
or, you know, I guess the more lazy fair,

00:29:21.148 --> 00:29:23.004
which I already get a sense that,

00:29:23.004 --> 00:29:24.518
or maybe not lazy fair,

00:29:24.518 --> 00:29:27.776
but you know, just following
the CDC guidelines,

00:29:27.776 --> 00:29:29.888
where would you be on,
I guess one of those

00:29:29.888 --> 00:29:31.688
first two options is my gut sense

00:29:31.688 --> 00:29:33.211
of where you would recommend.

00:29:34.066 --> 00:29:35.838
- I think at this point,

00:29:35.838 --> 00:29:39.598
just from the initial few weeks
of university experiences,

00:29:39.598 --> 00:29:41.656
or even now it's up to
a month in some places

00:29:41.656 --> 00:29:43.728
as you know, athletes have come back,

00:29:43.728 --> 00:29:45.958
I would lean in favor of really

00:29:45.958 --> 00:29:47.906
extensive testing on arrival.

00:29:47.906 --> 00:29:50.902
And then with some frequency
over the course of semester.

00:29:50.902 --> 00:29:52.458
It may be too much

00:29:52.458 --> 00:29:54.848
and maybe we can pull back
from that as we learn more.

00:29:54.848 --> 00:29:57.148
But I think there've
been a number of colleges

00:29:57.148 --> 00:29:58.618
and universities where we've seen

00:29:58.618 --> 00:30:02.708
that doing less is not a good option

00:30:02.708 --> 00:30:07.648
and where schools have had to be closed.

00:30:07.648 --> 00:30:09.658
Colleges would be closed after
two weeks of being opened

00:30:09.658 --> 00:30:11.458
after they've had 100s of new cases.

00:30:12.758 --> 00:30:13.938
- And, and so you would...

00:30:13.938 --> 00:30:15.868
if you're a university
president you would rather open

00:30:15.868 --> 00:30:17.134
and do the aggressive testing than just,

00:30:17.134 --> 00:30:18.575
you know, stay distance learning?

00:30:18.575 --> 00:30:20.408
(indistinct)

00:30:20.408 --> 00:30:21.541
- Yeah, I think at this point,

00:30:21.541 --> 00:30:26.096
in terms of overall
virtual versus in person,

00:30:26.096 --> 00:30:28.672
I think some parts of the country

00:30:28.672 --> 00:30:33.261
have had a community
incidence, which is pretty low,

00:30:34.114 --> 00:30:36.658
like for example, many
places in the Northeast,

00:30:36.658 --> 00:30:38.748
their overall daily incidents of cases

00:30:38.748 --> 00:30:41.468
in that particular city
is somewhere in the order

00:30:41.468 --> 00:30:45.166
of three, four, five
cases per day, per 100000.

00:30:45.166 --> 00:30:46.814
Other parts of the country,

00:30:46.814 --> 00:30:49.548
the incidence of disease is more like

00:30:49.548 --> 00:30:54.398
20 or 30 or 40 or 50
cases per day, per 100000.

00:30:54.398 --> 00:30:57.088
That's a very dramatic difference.

00:30:57.088 --> 00:31:02.088
And so what I hope is
that university towns,

00:31:02.692 --> 00:31:05.464
college towns, the communities around them

00:31:05.464 --> 00:31:08.008
are able to drive their numbers down

00:31:08.008 --> 00:31:09.538
to a low enough incidence

00:31:09.538 --> 00:31:13.161
where the arrival of
students is not a threat

00:31:13.161 --> 00:31:16.082
and doesn't threat to
kind of be a resurgence

00:31:16.082 --> 00:31:19.571
and the town itself isn't
a threat to the students.

00:31:19.571 --> 00:31:21.518
The kind of the imbalance.

00:31:21.518 --> 00:31:22.518
Right now, I think there are,

00:31:22.518 --> 00:31:24.834
if you draw from across the country,

00:31:24.834 --> 00:31:26.498
we do have a lot of places right now,

00:31:26.498 --> 00:31:27.898
which have very high incidents

00:31:27.898 --> 00:31:31.448
and those students, if they're
gonna come across country

00:31:31.448 --> 00:31:33.408
to a town, then I think that university

00:31:33.408 --> 00:31:35.618
has a real obligation to test them

00:31:35.618 --> 00:31:37.492
and make sure that they're not bringing in

00:31:37.492 --> 00:31:40.038
kind of higher risk for that community.

00:31:40.038 --> 00:31:42.124
So I would say, it's a long answer to say,

00:31:42.124 --> 00:31:44.699
in some places, I think
it was reasonable to open

00:31:44.699 --> 00:31:47.618
and other places where the
incidence is very high,

00:31:47.618 --> 00:31:49.234
it was probably higher risk

00:31:49.234 --> 00:31:51.234
and I'm not sure I would have done that.

00:31:52.258 --> 00:31:55.408
- Yeah and I realize
we're already out of time,

00:31:55.408 --> 00:31:56.539
this is super helpful.

00:31:56.539 --> 00:31:58.551
You know, just last thoughts on,

00:31:58.551 --> 00:32:00.278
I mean, it sounds like, you know,

00:32:00.278 --> 00:32:01.918
we're hopefully all fingers crossed

00:32:01.918 --> 00:32:04.858
that the vaccine research,
the phase three trials,

00:32:04.858 --> 00:32:06.028
and then the distribution works

00:32:06.028 --> 00:32:07.158
over the next six, seven months.

00:32:07.158 --> 00:32:10.188
It seems like the most likely scenario.

00:32:10.188 --> 00:32:14.013
If you were, you know, emperor
of the universe right now,

00:32:14.013 --> 00:32:17.203
would you just be kinda banking on that?

00:32:17.203 --> 00:32:21.086
Or are there other things
that you think we could do?

00:32:21.086 --> 00:32:22.498
You know, there's some stuff that's maybe

00:32:22.498 --> 00:32:23.668
a little bit water under the bridge,

00:32:23.668 --> 00:32:25.338
obviously, if we had
better contact tracing,

00:32:25.338 --> 00:32:27.257
if we had better testing earlier on,

00:32:27.257 --> 00:32:29.488
but are there other
things that we could do

00:32:29.488 --> 00:32:32.054
that could give us a more likelihood

00:32:32.054 --> 00:32:34.553
of getting to normalcy faster?

00:32:34.553 --> 00:32:38.198
- Yeah, I mean, there are
other parts of the world,

00:32:38.198 --> 00:32:40.458
which are much closer to normal.

00:32:40.458 --> 00:32:42.383
I mean, I would say no country

00:32:42.383 --> 00:32:44.499
is acting completely normally now.

00:32:44.499 --> 00:32:49.408
And probably every country has
a level of economic downturn

00:32:49.408 --> 00:32:51.591
and challenge and societal challenge,

00:32:51.591 --> 00:32:53.781
but there are parts of the world

00:32:53.781 --> 00:32:56.362
where they've had very few deaths

00:32:56.362 --> 00:32:58.278
in the last couple of months,

00:32:58.278 --> 00:33:00.243
very few cases in the
last couple of months,

00:33:00.243 --> 00:33:02.548
and they're doing the
things that we're doing,

00:33:02.548 --> 00:33:07.480
but they're doing them with
more collective buy-in,

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:09.507
with more unity of message.

00:33:09.507 --> 00:33:14.001
I think we've just had
kind of a messy six months

00:33:16.484 --> 00:33:19.278
where some states have acted one way,

00:33:19.278 --> 00:33:20.608
some states have acted another.

00:33:20.608 --> 00:33:22.408
We've reopened quickly

00:33:22.408 --> 00:33:24.212
and then had to shut down again.

00:33:24.212 --> 00:33:26.726
If we kind of continue on what we know,

00:33:26.726 --> 00:33:30.673
which is physical distancing,
universal masking,

00:33:30.673 --> 00:33:33.938
when you're outside and near other people,

00:33:33.938 --> 00:33:37.469
avoiding large gatherings,
basic hand hygiene,

00:33:37.469 --> 00:33:40.260
stay home when you're sick,
get tested if you're ill,

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:42.818
do the things we talked
about in this half hour,

00:33:42.818 --> 00:33:45.008
I think those things
will go a very long way.

00:33:45.008 --> 00:33:47.008
Even if we don't have a vaccine,

00:33:47.008 --> 00:33:48.899
I think we can get closer to normal

00:33:48.899 --> 00:33:50.788
and other countries have done that.

00:33:50.788 --> 00:33:53.148
So I think if we keep
moving in that direction,

00:33:53.148 --> 00:33:55.798
even before we have a vaccine,
we'll be doing better.

00:33:56.948 --> 00:33:59.158
- Awesome, well, thank you so much, Tom.

00:33:59.158 --> 00:34:01.552
This was a super useful update

00:34:01.552 --> 00:34:03.318
on where we are in this journey.

00:34:03.318 --> 00:34:05.548
It all makes a ton of
sense, thank you for this.

00:34:05.548 --> 00:34:07.595
- And thanks for having me, Sal.

00:34:07.595 --> 00:34:08.428
- All right.

00:34:08.428 --> 00:34:10.649
Well, thanks everyone for joining.

00:34:10.649 --> 00:34:14.775
I hope you got as much out of
that conversation as I did.

00:34:14.775 --> 00:34:18.811
Special thanks to my wife for
cutting my hair yesterday.

00:34:18.811 --> 00:34:21.378
It was getting a little bit outlandish,

00:34:21.378 --> 00:34:24.558
but it's still looking a little bit odd.

00:34:24.558 --> 00:34:27.104
